    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=ac3ea3a3-1795-4ceb-80c1-668296dbec35&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201188991214_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assist Professor, Health and Wellness Coachin",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-06-15\n\nHealth and Wellness Coaching  - Assistant Professor and Curriculum Manager Position Description Annual Salary: $62,000-$64,000/year   The full-time, non-tenure track Assistant Professor in Health and Wellness Coaching who will also serve as Curriculum Manager for the institution's health and wellness coaching programs. This faculty position combines teaching, scholarship, and service with primary responsibility for leading the creation, review, development, and alignment of coaching curriculum to meet the accreditation standards of the National Board for Health & Wellness Coaching (NBHWC) and the International Coaching Federation (ICF).   Core Responsibilities   Curriculum Leadership Lead design, development, and continuous improvement of health and wellness coaching curricula across certificate and degree programs. Create and maintain curriculum maps that explicitly align learning outcomes, course content, learning activities, and assessments with NBHWC and ICF core competencies and accreditation requirements. Oversee course sequencing, prerequisites, and program pathways to ensure coherence, progression, and competency attainment. Accreditation and Quality Assurance Serve as a liaison for NBHWC and ICF accreditation and credentialing processes. Prepare and maintain accreditation documentation, program self-studies, and evidence of compliance. Implement program evaluation systems, collect and analyze outcomes data, and lead corrective action and continuous improvement efforts. Instruction and Supervision Teach undergraduate and/or graduate courses in health and wellness coaching, behavior change, motivational interviewing, ethics, and related topics. Supervise coaching practicums, clinical hours, and student coaching portfolios to ensure students meet competency and supervised practice requirements. Provide individual and group coaching supervision and mentor faculty and adjunct instructors in coaching pedagogy and assessment. Faculty and Stakeholder Development Develop and deliver faculty development workshops on coaching competencies, assessment methods, and best practices in experiential learning. Coordinate with instructional designers and educational technologists to integrate active learning, simulation, and LMS tools. Scholarship and Service Maintain an active program of scholarly activity related to coaching education, behavior change, program evaluation, or related fields. Participate in departmental, college, and university service, including committees related to curriculum, accreditation, and assessment. Teaching, Scholarship, and Service Expectations Teaching load:  Typical load of 24 credit hours per academic year, with some course release time considered for Curriculum Manager responsibilities. Scholarship:  Demonstrated or emerging record of peer-reviewed publications, presentations, or applied research; expectation to pursue external funding as appropriate. Service:  Active participation in program governance, accreditation activities, and university and community engagement. Required Qualifications Terminal degree  (PhD, EdD, DNP, DrPH, or equivalent) in health and wellness coaching, health education, public health, counseling, psychology, coaching science, education, or a closely related field; or a master's degree plus substantial, documented professional experience in coaching education and program leadership. NBHWC certification  and ICF credential  (PCC or MCC). Demonstrated experience in curriculum design,  curriculum mapping, or instructional design for competency-based programs. Knowledge of or experience aligning programs to professional standards and accreditation processes. Experience teaching or supervising health and wellness coaches, including practicum or clinical supervision. Strong written and oral communication skills and demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with faculty, staff, and external partners. Preferred Qualifications Prior experience serving as an accreditation liaison or leading an accreditation/self-study process. Experience with competency-based assessment, program evaluation, and learning analytics. Familiarity with adult learning theory, experiential learning methods, and coaching supervision models. Experience with learning management systems, simulation, or digital coaching platforms. Records of scholarly productivity in coaching, behavior change, education, or related applied research. Please send all resumes with a cover letter to the attention of Duston Morris at dmorris@muih.ndm.edu “Additional Information: Notre Dame of Maryland University does not discriminate in offering equal access to its educational programs and activities or with respect to employment terms and conditions on the basis of age, color, creed, disability, genetic information, marital status, national or ethnic origin or ancestry, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy, sexual orientation, or gender identity or expression) or veteran status or any other characteristic protected in accordance with applicable federal, state, and local laws and regulations. The following individual has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the University’s non-discrimination policy : Greg FitzGerald, Title IX/504/ADA Coordinator, Notre Dame of Maryland University, 4701 North Charles Street, Baltimore, MD 21210, (410) 532-5109. For all other employment inquiries, please contact Human Resources at 410-532-5898 or human_resources@ndm.edu . Notre Dame of Maryland University is an EEO employer.”",
      "department": ""
    },
